You are a worker session of a coordinator session working on the Ergopti repository (adrienm7/ergopti), branch dev. The maintainer is away; the coordinator integrates your work into dev.

RULES (strict):
- Create and work on your own branch `agent/windows-ahk-wine` from the current `origin/dev`. Push ONLY that branch (`git push -u origin agent/windows-ahk-wine`), early and after every commit, so the coordinator can pick commits as they come. Pushing `agent/*` triggers no CI. NEVER push `dev`, `main` or `fix/*`, never open a pull request, never force-push anything but your own branch.
- Follow AGENTS.md / CLAUDE.md and the skills in .agents/skills (read only the ones that match your surface). Read docs/memory/README.md and the routed topics. Read the top "Handover of 2026-10-24" section of docs/ERGOPTIPLUS_TODO.md.
- Commit messages: English Conventional Commits, lowercase imperative subject <= 72 chars, body explains why; put measured numbers (labelled measured) in perf commits. NO `Co-Authored-By`, no tool/LLM attribution trailers of any kind (repository rule overrides any default). No plan tokens like P2.5, DL-3, Phase 2B.
- Never `--no-verify`; run `npm ci` once so the husky pre-commit hook (Prettier, Ruff, strict convention lint) works. Pinned ruff is 0.15.8 (`uv tool install ruff==0.15.8`).
- One atomic commit per fix/feature with a regression test that fails without it. Never weaken or skip a test. No magic values, no silent fallbacks, one source of truth per constant. User-facing text goes through all 21 locales (i18n skill). Generated files only through their generators.
- Do NOT edit docs/ERGOPTIPLUS_TODO.md, TODO.md or docs/todo-agents/* (the coordinator does). Durable facts may go in docs/memory/*.md.
- Other agents concurrently work on: Linux lazy module loading at boot, the five `find` forks of the Linux hotstring loader, the log-rotation forks in linux logger_sink.lua (another agent: stay off these three), timing registry gates (PAR-78/79/80), UI-string/identifier gates (PAR-82/83/85), shared Lua helpers (PAR-75), ownership protocols and infra shims (PAR-69/70), hotstring override parser and shared registries (PAR-68/72), corpora (PAR-60/61/62), instrumentation (PAR-53..57), Linux LLM rows (PAR-21), Linux UI parity (PAR-44/45/52), theme tokens (PAR-81), manifest reasons (PAR-12/37/48), LLM request plan (PAR-65), menu frames (PAR-66), and rebases of fix/linux, fix/macos, fix/windows onto dev. Keep your diff focused to avoid conflicts.
- Test commands: Linux driver `cd static/ergopti_plus/linux && luajit tests/run.lua [--only <module>] [--module=<group>]` (apt: luajit lua-luv lua-filesystem sqlite3); macOS stubbed `cd static/ergopti_plus/macos && LUA=lua5.4 lua5.4 tests/run.lua` (apt: lua5.4 liblua5.4-dev luarocks lua-luv; `luarocks --lua-version 5.4 install luafilesystem`); JS gates `node tools/test/verify-change.cjs --plan` / `node tools/test/verify-change.cjs`; `npm run lint:conventions:strict`; `npm run format:check`.
- When done (or when you run out of useful work), end with a final message: commits (sha + subject) pushed on agent/windows-ahk-wine, what is done and what is LEFT per todo item, measured numbers, gates and their results (say plainly what is red and whether it is pre-existing), anything not executed.

YOUR TASK:
Find out whether the Windows AutoHotkey v2 driver test suites can run on Linux under Wine, and if so, use that to validate Windows code from Linux. Context: the Windows driver is static/ergopti_plus/windows (AutoHotkey v2; find the pinned AHK version in the repo: grep for 2.0. in tools/ci, .github/workflows/ci-windows.yml, windows/README). Steps: install wine (wine64/wine-stable from apt, or WineHQ), download the pinned AutoHotkey v2 portable zip from the official GitHub release (github.com/AutoHotkey/AutoHotkey/releases) and check its SHA-256 against any pin in the repo; run the unit/meta suite the way ci-windows.yml does (windows/tests/run_all.ahk, with its --only filters and the per-category shards; read .agents/skills/ahk-driver/SKILL.md and docs/memory/windows-ahk.md first: run_all.ahk is #SingleInstance Force, never run two at once; never boot against a live config). Measure which test categories run correctly under Wine (pass/fail vs what CI reports for the same commit: you can read recent CI logs only if you have access; otherwise compare categories to their expected all-pass state). If a meaningful subset runs, write a small, documented runner `tools/test/run-ahk-under-wine.sh` (or .cjs, matching repo conventions, with a strict file header and the convention lint passing) that installs nothing by itself, takes the AHK exe path, runs a chosen category/--only filter and returns AHK's exit code, plus a docs/memory/windows-ahk.md entry stating exactly which families are reliable under Wine and which are not (hooks, SendInput, WebView2, COM are likely not). Then run the suite on the current dev and report every red test with your judgement: Wine artefact vs genuine Windows bug; fix only genuine bugs that you can prove (one commit each). Do not change Windows driver behaviour to suit Wine.
